<article class="env-lemma" id="04R9">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/04R9">Lemma <span data-tag="04R9">40.4.1</span>.</a> Let $S$ be a scheme. Let $(U, R, s, t, c)$ be a groupoid scheme over $S$. The sheaf of differentials of $R$ seen as a scheme over $U$ via $t$ is a quotient of the pullback via $t$ of the conormal sheaf of the immersion $e : U \to R$. In a formula: there is a canonical surjection $t^*\mathcal{C}_{U/R} \to \Omega _{R/U}$. If $s$ is flat, then this map is an isomorphism. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      Note that $e : U \to R$ is an immersion as it is a section of the morphism $s$, see Schemes, Lemma <a href="/tag/01KT" data-tag="01KT">26.21.11</a>. Consider the following diagram 
    <p>
      <div class="equation">
  \[  \xymatrix{ R \ar[r]_-{(1, i)} \ar[d]_ t &  R \times _{s, U, t} R \ar[d]^ c \ar[rr]_{(\text{pr}_0, i \circ \text{pr}_1)} &  &  R \times _{t, U, t} R \\  U \ar[r]^ e &  R }  \]
</div>
    <p>
       The square on the left is cartesian, because if $a \circ b = e$, then $b = i(a)$. The composition of the horizontal maps is the diagonal morphism of $t : R \to U$. The right top horizontal arrow is an isomorphism. Hence since $\Omega _{R/U}$ is the conormal sheaf of the composition it is isomorphic to the conormal sheaf of $(1, i)$. By Morphisms, Lemma <a href="/tag/0473" data-tag="0473">29.32.4</a> we get the surjection $t^*\mathcal{C}_{U/R} \to \Omega _{R/U}$ and if $c$ is flat, then this is an isomorphism. Since $c$ is a base change of $s$ by the properties of Diagram (<a href="/tag/04LG" data-tag="04LG">40.3.0.2</a>) we conclude that if $s$ is flat, then $c$ is flat, see Morphisms, Lemma <a href="/tag/01U9" data-tag="01U9">29.26.8</a>. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>